{% extends 'shells/docs.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}Actions · php-via Docs{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article>
    <h1>Actions</h1>
    <p>
        Actions are server-side event handlers that the browser can trigger. When a user clicks a button or submits a form,
        Datastar POSTs to the action's URL and your PHP callback runs: signals update, views re-render, and patches stream back via SSE.
    </p>

    <h2 id="creating">Creating an action</h2>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$increment = $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($count): void {
    $count->setValue($count->int() + 1);
    $c->sync();
}, 'increment');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>
        The first argument is the callback; the second is an optional name used in the URL.
        The name defaults to an auto-generated ID if omitted, but a name makes debugging easier.
    </p>

    <h2 id="url">Action URL</h2>
    <p>Get the URL to pass into your Twig template:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$increment->url();  // '/_action/increment'
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>In Twig, bind it to a Datastar event attribute:</p>
    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<button data-on:click="@post('{{ increment_url }}')">>+1</button>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <div class="callout callout--warn">
        <strong>Always use <code>@post()</code> for actions.</strong>
        <code>GET /_action/…</code> returns <strong>405 Method Not Allowed</strong>.
        Accepting actions over GET would allow top-level browser navigation (a link or redirect from any
        origin) to trigger state changes without the user's intent, a classic
        <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/csrf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CSRF</a> vector.
        Use <code>@post()</code>, <code>@patch()</code>, <code>@put()</code>, or <code>@delete()</code>:
        all of these require an explicit browser <code>fetch()</code> call, which the browser's
        cross-origin policy controls. <code>@get()</code> is fine for the SSE endpoint
        (<code>/_sse</code>), just not for actions.
    </div>

    <h2 id="post-data">Reading POST data</h2>
    <p>Read POST body and query parameters with <code>$c->input()</code> inside the callback. OpenSwoole does not fill <code>$_POST</code> or <code>$_GET</code>:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$vote = $c->action(function (Context $c) use ($app, $votes): void {
    $option = $c->input('option');
    if (!in_array($option, ['a', 'b', 'c'], true)) {
        return; // ignore unknown values
    }
    $app->incrementGlobalState('vote_' . $option);
    $votes->setValue(['a' => $app->globalState('vote_a'), 'b' => $app->globalState('vote_b')]);
    $app->broadcast(Scope::routeScope('/'));
}, 'vote');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    {% code 'html' %}{% verbatim %}
<!-- Pass option as query param -->
<button data-on:click="@post('{{ vote_url }}?option=a')">Vote A</button>
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="syncing">Syncing after an action</h2>
    <p>
        Actions don't auto-sync. You must call <code>$c-&gt;sync()</code> (view + signals) or <code>$c-&gt;syncSignals()</code>
        (signals only, faster) to push changes to the browser:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->action(function (Context $c) use ($label): void {
    $label->setValue('Saved!');
    $c->syncSignals(); // only the signal changed: skip view re-render
}, 'save');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <p>
        For ROUTE-scoped signals, use <code>$app-&gt;broadcast()</code> instead so <em>all</em> clients on the route receive the update.
        See <a href="/docs/broadcasting">Broadcasting</a> for details.
    </p>

    <h2 id="multiple-actions">Multiple actions per context</h2>
    <p>A context can have as many actions as needed. Each gets its own URL:</p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$inc   = $c->action(fn(Context $c) use ($count) => $count->setValue($count->int() + 1) & $c->sync(), 'inc');
$dec   = $c->action(fn(Context $c) use ($count) => $count->setValue($count->int() - 1) & $c->sync(), 'dec');
$reset = $c->action(fn(Context $c) use ($count) => $count->setValue(0) & $c->sync(), 'reset');
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}
    <p>
        Registering a second TAB action under the same name in one context replaces the first and logs a
        warning. A scoped action keeps its first registration.
    </p>

    <h2 id="errors">When an action throws</h2>
    <p>
        A throw from an action, a view or a timer never takes the worker down. An action that throws any
        <code>\Throwable</code> answers 500 <code>Action failed</code>, the worker keeps running with
        whatever the action changed before it threw, and the error is logged with its class, message and
        <code>file:line</code>. A page whose handler or render throws answers 500 and its context is torn
        down. A view that throws during a broadcast skips that one tab, and the other tabs still get the
        frame. A per-tab <code>$c-&gt;setInterval()</code> keeps ticking after a throw.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cleanup">Cleanup actions</h2>
    <p>
        For actions that should only run once, or that need cleanup when the user leaves,
        combine with <a href="/docs/lifecycle">lifecycle hooks</a>:
    </p>
    {% code 'php' %}{% verbatim %}
$c->onDisconnect(function (Context $c) use ($room, $app): void {
    Room::$members[$room]--;
    $app->broadcast('room:' . $room);
});
    {% endverbatim %}{% endcode %}

    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="/docs/signals">Signals</a>: the state that actions modify</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/broadcasting">Broadcasting</a>: pushing updates to multiple clients</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/views">Views</a>: how <code>sync()</code> triggers a view re-render</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/lifecycle">Lifecycle</a>: cleanup hooks and timers</li>
    </ul>
</article>

{% endblock %}
